#
# S25 port of testkit's scenarios/fd-starve against a real cache_turbo HIT
# path. Five idle client sockets remain open across the probe snapshot, under
# worker_connections 10. `connections.free <= 4` is the live pressure witness;
# the lower bound proves the scenario did not simply exhaust the probe itself.
#
# HONEST SCOPE, verified while building this file: cache_turbo's L1 is
# purely shm-backed (grep confirms no ngx_open_file/ngx_socket/
# ngx_open_tempfile/ngx_close_file anywhere in src/*.c) and a HIT touches no
# upstream connection at all, so there is no known code path in THIS module
# that can fail this oracle on a HIT today. What it DOES catch: a future
# module change that opens any descriptor on the HIT path and forgets to
# release it (the failure class the fd count exists to catch at all), and it
# is a real backstop rather than a no-op -- upstream's own ref-module form
# carries the identical caveat for the same structural reason. Wired as
# backstop coverage, not because a mutation was found to red it.

name    baseline: warm-up MISS under a tight connection table
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /cached/item HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect_not header~X-Cache:
probe   connections.free >= 6

name    five parked sockets apply measurable pressure during a cache HIT
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /cached/item HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  header~X-Cache: HIT
open_conns 5
probe   connections.free <= 4
probe   connections.free >= 1
delta   fds == 0

name    server remains graceful after descriptor pressure is released
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /cached/item HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  header~X-Cache: HIT
delta   fds == 0
